CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to contribute to the development of the agriculture, forestry and fishing industry by prescribing matters regarding the protection of the rights of breeders of new plant varieties.

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows: <Amended on Jun. 22, 2015>
  1. The term "seed" means the seed defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Seed Industry Act and seed of aquatic plants defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Fisheries Seed Industry Promotion Act;
  2. The term "plant variety" means a group of plants at the lowest classification unit commonly used in botany, which is distinguished from other groups of plants by at least one of its genetically expressed characteristics and which is capable of being propagated unchanged, irrespective of whether the requirements for the grant of a plant variety right under Article 16 are fully met;
  3. The term "breeder" means a person who breeds, or discovers and develops, a plant variety;
  4. The term "plant variety right" means a right granted to a person entitled to plant variety protection under this Act;
  5. The term "plant variety right holder" means the holder of a plant variety right;
  6. The term "protected plant variety" means a plant variety for which a plant variety right is granted as it meets requirements for plant variety protection under this Act;
  7. The term "exploitation" means the propagation, production, made-to-order production, transfer, loan, export, import, and application for transfer or loan (including exhibition for transfer or loan; hereinafter the same shall apply) of the seeds of protected plant varieties.

Article 3 (Plant varieties protectable) Plant varieties protectable under this Act shall be all plant varieties.

CHAPTER II PROTECTION OF RIGHTS OF BREEDERS

SECTION 1 Common Provisions

Article 4 (Managers of plant variety protection for expatriates) (1) Unless by a person who has his or her address or business office in the Republic of Korea and is the agent (hereinafter referred to as "manager of plant variety protection") of another person who has no address or business office in Korea (hereinafter referred to as "expatriate") concerning plant variety protection, neither procedures for plant variety protection shall be taken at the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries or the Plant Variety Protection Tribunal under Article 90(1) (hereinafter referred to as "procedures for plant variety protection") nor a lawsuit against disposition issued by administrative agencies under this Act or an order under this Act shall be filed, except where the expatriate applies for registration under paragraph (3) and in cases prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) A manager of plant variety protection shall represent the principal in lawsuits against disposition issued by an administrative agency under this Act or orders under this Act and all procedures for plant variety protection within the authority specifically given to him or her.
  (3) Unless an expatriate having a plant variety right or a right registered concerning plant variety protection files for registration of the appointment and change of a manager of variety protection or the granting or revocation of the agent's authority, as prescribed by the joint decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs and the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries (hereinafter referred to as "joint ministerial decree"), he or she shall not assert the effect thereof against a third party. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) An expatriate shall register the appointment or change of a manager of plant variety protection when he or she files for registration of the establishment of a plant variety right or while the relevant plant variety right is valid.

Article 5 (Scope of authority of agency) No agent delegated to take procedures for plant variety protection by a person having address or business office in Korea shall engage in any of the following unless he or she is given specific authority to do so:
  1. Change, abandonment or withdrawal of an application for plant variety protection;
  2. Withdrawal of requests or applications;
  3. Claim for the right of priority under Article 31(1) or withdrawal thereof;
  4. Requests for adjudication under Article 91;
  5. Appointment of a subagent.

Article 6 (Proof of authority of agency) The agent (including plant variety protection managers; hereinafter the same shall apply) of a person taking procedures for plant variety protection shall be verified in writing.

Article 7 (Representative of multiple parties) (1) Where at least two persons take procedures for plant variety protection, each one of them represents all of them except for the acts specified in subparagraphs 1 through 4 of Article 5; provided, the same shall not apply where they select a representative and file a report to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries [or the chairperson of the Plant Variety Protection Tribunal (hereinafter referred to as "chairperson of the Tribunal") under Article 90(2) in cases falling under subparagraph 4 of Article 5]. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The representative shall verify that he or she has been selected as a representative in writing when filing a report under the proviso of paragraph (1).

Article 8 (Extension of period) (1) For persons living in an area with poor transportation services, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the chairperson of the Tribunal may extend the period for applying for adjudication under Article 91 or the period for amending grounds for filing an objection against the registration of denominations of plant varieties under Article 111, etc., upon the request of the relevant applicant or ex officio. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Where a period during which procedures for plant variety protection are taken is set by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, the chairperson of the Tribunal, chief adjudicator designated under Article 95(2) (hereinafter referred to as "chief adjudicator") or an examiner referred to in Article 36 (hereinafter referred to as "examiner") under this Act, he or she may extend the period upon the request of the relevant applicant or ex officio. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) When a date by which procedures for plant variety protection shall be taken is set by a chief adjudicator or examiner under this Act, he or she may change the date upon the request of the relevant applicant or ex officio.

Article 9 (Amendment of procedures) Where any of the following applies to procedures for plant variety protection, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the chairperson of the Tribunal may issue an order for amendment specifying a period: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Where Article 5 is violated or Article 3(1) of the Patent Act applied mutatis mutandis under Article 15 is violated;
  2. Where methods determined by this Act or an order issued under this Act are not followed;
  3. Where fees due under Article 125 are not paid.

Article 10 (Invalidation of procedures) (1) Where a person subject to an order for amendment issued under Article 9 fails to make an amendment within a prescribed period, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the chairperson of the Tribunal may invalidate the procedures for plant variety protection. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Where procedures for plant variety protection are invalidated under paragraph (1), and a person subject to the order for amendment is deemed to have failed to comply within the prescribed period due to a natural disaster or other inevitable grounds, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the chairperson of the Tribunal may revoke the disposition of invalidation within 14 days from the date the relevant ground ceases to exist or within one year after the expiration of such period. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the chairperson of the Tribunal issues a disposition of invalidation under paragraph (1) or a disposition revoking the disposition of invalidation under paragraph (2), he or she shall, without delay, serve a notice of disposition on the person subject to the order for amendment. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 11 (When filings of documents become effective) (1) Applications, requests and other documents (including articles; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) submitted to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the chairperson of the Tribunal under this Act or an order issued under this Act shall take effect from the date they reach the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the chairperson of the Tribunal. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Where applications, requests and other documents referred to in paragraph (1) are submitted to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the chairperson of the Tribunal by mail, they shall be deemed to have reached the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the chairperson of the Tribunal on the date indicated on the postmark under postal statutes or regulations if such date is clearly marked, or on the date they are submitted to the post office (referring to the date shown on the postal receipt) if not clearly marked. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) and (2), matters regarding the submission of documents having problems due to delay in the delivery of mail, loss or suspension of postal service shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 12 (Implementation of procedures for plant variety protection using electronic documents) (1) A person who takes procedures for plant variety protection may submit an application for plant variety protection or other documents required under this Act to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the chairperson of the Tribunal, in electronic form, via an information and communications network or after recording them on the electronic recording media, such as portable storage devices. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Electronic documents submitted under paragraph (1) shall have the same effect as the documents submitted under this Act.
  (3) Electronic documents submitted via an information and communications network under paragraph (1) shall be deemed received at the time they are electronically recorded on the electronic information processing system for receiving documents, used by the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries or the Tribunal. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Kinds of documents that may be submitted in electronic form under paragraph (1), methods of submission and other matters necessary for submitting electronic documents shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 13 (Reporting on use of electronic documents and digital signature) (1) A person who intends to take procedures for plant variety protection in electronic documents pursuant to Article 12(1) shall first report on the use of electronic documents to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the chairperson of the Tribunal, and affix a digital signature on the electronic documents submitted so that the submitter can be identified. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Procedures for reporting on the use of electronic documents under paragraph (1), methods of affixing a digital signature, and other matters shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 14 (Notification via information and communications networks) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, the chairperson of the Tribunal, a chief adjudicator and an examiner may notify documents to or serve documents on a person who have reported on the use of electronic documents under Article 13(1) (hereinafter referred to as "notification, etc. of documents") via an information and communications network. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The notification, etc. of documents via an information and communications network under paragraph (1) shall have the same effect as those given in writing.
  (3) The notification, etc. of documents shall be deemed to have reached at the time they are electronically recorded on the electronic information processing system used by a recipient of such documents.
  (4) Matters regarding the kinds and methods of notification, etc. of documents via an information and communications network under paragraph (1) and other matters shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 15 (Application mutatis mutandis of Patent Act) `Articles 3, 4, 8, 9, 10(1), (2) and (4), 13, 14 and 17 through 24 of the Patent Act and Articles 58(2), 59, 63, 87, 88, 92, 94 and 96 of the Civil Procedure Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to procedures for plant variety protection. In such cases, "the location of the Ministry of Intellectual Property" referred to in Article 13 of the Patent Act shall be construed as "the location of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, and "Article 132-17" referred to in subparagraph 1 of Article 17 of that Act as "Article 91", respectively. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 29, 2016; Oct. 1, 2025>

SECTION 2 Requirements and Applications for Plant Variety Protection

Article 16 (Requirements for plant variety protection) A plant variety that meets the following requirements shall be entitled to plant variety protection under this Act:
  1. Novelty;
  2. Distinctness;
  3. Uniformity;
  4. Stability;
  5. Denomination of a plant variety under Article 106(1).

Article 17 (Novelty) (1) A plant variety shall be deemed new as provided for in subparagraph 1 of Article 16 if, as of the date of filing of the application for protection of the plant variety under Article 32(2) (the date of filing of the first application for protection of the plant variety if the right of priority is claimed under Article 31(1)), seeds or harvested material therefrom have not been transferred for purposes of exploitation for at least one year in Korea, and for at least four years (six years in the case of a fruit tree or forest tree) in other countries.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), a plant variety shall be deemed new as provided for in subparagraph 1 of Article 16 if:
  1. The seeds of a misappropriated plant variety or harvested material therefrom are transferred;
  2. The seeds of a plant variety or harvested material therefrom are transferred to transfer the right to plant variety protection;
  3. The breeder accepts the seeds of a plant variety or harvested material therefrom after having transferred the seeds of the plant variety or harvested material therefrom for purposes of propagating the seed and have them propagated;
  4. The seeds of a plant variety or harvested material therefrom are transferred for field cultivation experiments, quality inspections or small-scale processing experiments for the evaluation of the plant variety;
  5. The seeds of a plant variety or harvested material therefrom are transferred for inspections to preserve biological resources or for registration on the national catalog of varieties (hereinafter referred to as "catalog of varieties") under Article 15 of the Seed Industry Act;
  6. By-products or surplus products produced by an act falling under any of subparagraphs 3 through 5 are transferred without using the denomination of the relevant plant variety.

Article 18 (Distinctness) (1) A plant variety shall be deemed distinct as provided for in subparagraph 2 of Article 16 if it is clearly distinguishable from any other plant variety, the existence of which is publicly known as of the date of filing of the application for protection of the plant variety under Article 32(2) (the date of filing of the first application for protection of the plant variety if the right of priority is claimed under Article 31(1)).
  (2) A plant variety, the existence of which is publicly known as referred to in paragraph (1) means any of the following plant varieties; provided, where a plant variety, the existence of which is publicly known contrary to the will of the person entitled to plant variety protection, such plant variety shall be excluded: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. A plant variety in circulation;
  2. A protected plant variety;
  3. A plant variety registered on the catalog of varieties;
  4. A plant variety registered with an association related to the seed industry prescribed by the joint ministerial decree.
  (3) In cases falling under paragraph (2)2 or 3, a plant variety for which an application is filed to protect the plant variety or to be registered on the catalog of varieties shall be deemed a plant variety, the existence of which is publicly known from the date of filing of the application; provided, a plant variety that is not protected under this Act or is not registered on the catalog of varieties shall be excluded.

Article 19 (Uniformity) A plant variety shall be deemed uniform as provided for in subparagraph 3 of Article 16 if, subject to the variation that may be expected from the particular features of its propagation, its essential characteristics are sufficiently uniform.

Article 20 (Stability) A plant variety shall be deemed stable as provided for in subparagraph 4 of Article 16 if its essential characteristics remain unchanged after repeated propagation (at the end of each cycle of propagation in the case of a particular cycle of propagation, such as a first filial generation hybrid).

Article 21 (Persons entitled to plant variety protection) (1) A breeder or his or her successor shall be entitled to plant variety protection, as prescribed by this Act.
  (2) If at least two breeders jointly breed a plant variety, a right to plant variety protection shall be jointly owned.

Article 22 (Legal capacity of foreigners) An alien among expatriates shall be entitled to a plant variety right or a right to plant variety protection only if:
  1. A country to which the alien belongs recognizes a plant variety right or a right to plant variety protection for nationals of the Republic of Korea under the same conditions as for the nationals of such country;
  2. A country to which the alien belongs recognizes a plant variety right or a right to plant variety protection for nationals of the Republic of Korea under the same conditions as for nationals of such country if the Republic of Korea recognizes a plant variety right or a right to plant variety protection for the alien;
  3. A plant variety right or a right to plant variety protection is recognized pursuant to treaties or those corresponding thereto (hereinafter referred to as "treaties, etc.").

Article 23 (Applications for plant variety protection by persons without rights and protection of legitimate right holders) Where a person who is not a successor to a right to plant variety protection or a person who deceives others into believing that a right to plant variety protection belongs to him or her (hereinafter referred to as "person without rights") files an application for protection of a plant variety, an application for plant variety protection filed thereafter by a legitimate right holder shall be deemed filed at the time the person without rights files an application for plant variety protection; provided, this shall not apply where an application for plant variety protection is filed after 30 days from the date the person without rights is served a certified copy of the decision of rejection pursuant to Article 42(3). <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>

Article 24 (Plant variety protection for persons without rights and protection of legitimate right holders) Where a trial ruling to invalidate plant variety protection on the grounds provided in Article 92(1)2 becomes final and conclusive, an application for plant variety protection filed by a legitimate right holder after filing of such application for plant variety protection shall be deemed filed at the time of such application for plant variety protection which becomes invalid is filed; provided, this shall not apply where an application for plant variety protection is filed after two years from the date such plant variety protection is published in the Official Gazette pursuant to Article 54(4) or after 30 days from the date such trial ruling becomes final and conclusive. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>

Article 25 (First-to-file rule) (1) Where at least two applications for plant variety protection for the same plant variety are filed on different days, only the person who first files an application for plant variety protection shall be entitled to plant variety protection.
  (2) Where at least two applications for plant variety protection for the same plant variety are filed on the same day, a person determined by those who intend to obtain plant variety protection (hereinafter referred to as "applicant for plant variety protection") by agreement shall be entitled to plant variety protection. In such cases, where no agreement is reached or reaching an agreement is impossible, no applicant for plant variety protection shall be entitled to protection of the relevant plant variety.
  (3) Where an application for plant variety protection become invalid or is withdrawn, such application for plant variety protection shall be deemed not to have existed ab initio for purposes of paragraph (1) or (2).
  (4) An application for plant variety protection filed by a person who is neither breeder nor successor to a right to plant variety protection shall be deemed not to have existed ab initio for purposes of paragraph (1) or (2).
  (5) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall order an applicant for plant variety protection to report the results of agreement within a prescribed period in cases falling under paragraph (2); and where no report is filed within such period, no agreement shall be deemed reached under paragraph (2). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 26 (Transfer of right to plant variety protection) (1) A right to plant variety protection may be transferred.
  (2) A right to plant variety protection shall not be the subject of a pledge.
  (3) Where a right to plant variety protection is jointly owned, none of the joint owners shall transfer his or her share without the consent of the other joint owners.

Article 27 (Succession to right to plant variety protection) (1) A person who has succeeded to a right to protection of the relevant plant variety before filing of an application for plant variety protection shall not assert his or her right against a third party unless he or she files an application for protection of the plant variety.
  (2) Where at least two applications for plant variety protection are filed on the same day for a right to the same plant variety protection inherited from the same person, it shall be effective only for the person designated upon agreement among the applicants for plant variety protection.
  (3) Where an applicant for plant variety protection fails to report on the change of title, succession to a right to plant variety protection after filing of an application for plant variety protection shall not take effect except in the case of inheritance or other general succession.
  (4) In the event of inheritance or other general succession of the right to plant variety protection, the successor shall, without delay, report the intent to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, as prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) Where at least two reports are filed on the inheritance of a right to plant variety protection inherited from the same person on the same day, it shall be effective only for a person designated upon agreement among those who have filed a report.
  (6) Article 25(5) shall apply mutatis mutandis to paragraphs (2) and (5).

Article 28 (Breeding by public officials as official duty) (1) Where a plant variety bred by a public official falls within the scope of duties of the State or a local government and the breeding of the plant variety belongs to the current or former duties of the public official (hereinafter referred to as "breeding as official duty"), the public official's right to protection of the relevant plant variety shall be succeeded to by the State or the local government; provided, in the case of breeding by teachers and staff members of national schools or public schools under the Higher Education Act in the course of duty, such right shall be succeeded to by an organization exclusively responsible for technical transfer and commercialization established under Article 11(1) of the Technology Transfer and Commercialization Promotion Act (hereinafter referred to as "exclusively responsible organization").
  (2) The disposal and management of a right to plant variety protection to which the State succeeds under paragraph (1) shall be governed by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, notwithstanding Article 8 of the State Property Act. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Matters necessary for the disposal and management of a right to plant variety protection under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 29 (Compensation for public officials for breeding as official duty) (1) Where the State, local government or exclusively responsible organization succeeds to a plant variety bred by a public official as official duty pursuant to Article 28(1), it shall pay just compensation to the public official.
  (2) Standards for compensation under paragraph (1), methods of payment and other matters necessary for compensation shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 30 (Application for plant variety protection) (1) An applicant for plant variety protection shall enter the following matters in an application for plant variety protection determined by the joint ministerial decree and file it with the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Name and address of the applicant for plant variety protection (in the case of a corporation, its name, name of its representative and the location of its business office);
  2. Name and address of the agent or the location of his or her place of business, if the applicant for plant variety protection is represented by an agent;
  3. Name and address of the breeder;
  4. Scientific name or common name of the plant to which the plant variety belongs;
  5. Denomination of the plant variety;
  6. Year, month and date of filing;
  7. Matters referred to in Article 31(3) (only applicable where the right of priority is claimed).
  (2) An application for plant variety protection filed under paragraph (1) shall be accompanied by the following: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. A description of the characteristics of the plant variety and process of breeding thereof;
  2. Photographs of the plant variety;
  3. Seed samples; in such cases, where seed samples are saplings, trophosomes or aquatic plants, the timing and method for submission hereof and other matters shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree;
  4. A certificate proving the payment of fees for filing the application for plant variety protection.
  (3) Where a right to plant variety protection is jointly owned under Article 21(2), all joint owners shall file an application for plant variety protection.
  (4) Matters necessary for descriptions under paragraph (2)1 shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 31 (Claim for the right of priority) (1) Where a national of a foreign country that recognizes an ROK national's the right of priority to an application for plant variety protection claims the right of priority after filing an application for plant variety protection in the Republic of Korea for the same plant variety as that he or she filed an application for plant variety protection in the foreign country, the filing date of the application for plant variety protection in such foreign country shall be deemed the filing date of the application for plant variety protection in the Republic of Korea for purposes of Article 25. The same shall apply where an ROK national files an application for plant variety protection in a foreign country that recognizes the right of priority to an application for plant variety protection and files an application for plant variety protection in the Republic of Korea.
  (2) A person who intends to claim the right of priority pursuant to paragraph (1) shall not be entitled to do so unless he or she files an application for plant variety protection within one year from the date following the filing date of the first application for plant variety protection.
  (3) A person who intends to claim the right of priority pursuant to paragraph (1) shall indicate in the application for plant variety protection the intent thereof, the name of the country in which the first application for plant variety protection is filed and the filing date of the first application for plant variety protection.
  (4) A person who has claimed the right of priority pursuant to paragraph (3) shall submit a certified copy of the application for plant variety protection recognized by the government of a country in which the first application for plant variety protection is filed within 90 days from the filing date of an application for plant variety protection under Article 32(2).
  (5) A person who has claimed the right of priority pursuant to paragraph (3) may request the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries to postpone the examination of the relevant plant variety for three years from the filing date of the first application for plant variety protection, and the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall accept the request unless good cause exists; provided, where the person who has claimed the right of priority abandons the first application for plant variety protection or a decision of rejection by the country in which an application for plant variety protection filed becomes final and conclusive, the plant variety may be examined even before the postponed date of examination of the application for plant variety protection at the request of the person who has claimed the right of priority. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 32 (Acceptance of applications) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall, without delay, accept an application for plant variety protection for a plant variety applied for plant variety protection pursuant to Article 30(1) (hereinafter referred to as "applied plant variety"), and where the application for plant variety protection satisfies all the requirements under Article 30 and amended on the grounds specified in subparagraph 2 of Article 9, he or she shall have it registered in the register of applications for plant variety protection prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The date of acceptance of an application for plant variety protection under paragraph (1) shall be deemed the filing date of the application for plant variety protection.

Article 33 (Amendment of applications) (1) An applicant for plant variety protection may amend an application for plant variety protection within the extent that does not change the intent of the contents originally entered in the application for plant variety protection by the deadline applicable as follows:
  1. Where a notice of the grounds for rejection is issued under Article 42: The period for submitting a written opinion on the notice of the grounds for rejection;
  2. Where a decision of plant variety protection is made under Article 43: Before a certified copy of the decision on plant variety protection is served;
  3. Where an application for adjudication against a decision of rejection is lodged under Article 91: Within 30 days therefrom.
  (2) Methods of amending applications for plant variety protection under paragraph (1) and other matters shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 34 (Exclusion from change of intent of application) Where any of the following applies to an amendment made under Article 33, the intent of an application for plant variety protection shall be deemed unchanged:
  1. Where typos are corrected;
  2. Where ambiguous contents are clarified;
  3. Other cases prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 35 (Dismissal of amendment) (1) If an amendment made after filing an application for plant variety protection changes the intent of the application, an examiner shall reject the amendment by decision, and notify the applicant for plant variety protection of such fact without delay.
  (2) The decision of rejection under paragraph (1) shall be made in writing and the grounds for such decision shall be stated.
  (3) No objection shall be filed against a decision of rejection made under paragraph (1); provided, this shall not apply where the decision of rejection is pending in adjudication under Article 91.

SECTION 3 Examination

Article 36 (Examination by examiners) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall require examiners to examine applications for plant variety protection filed under Article 30 and applications for the registration of denominations of plant varieties filed under Article 109. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Matters necessary for qualifications of examiners shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 37 (Laying-open of applications) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall publish applications for plant variety protection entered in the register of applications for plant variety protection under Article 32(1) in the Official Gazette of plant variety protection under Article 53 (hereinafter referred to as "Official Gazette") for the applications to be laid-open. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Where applications are laid-open under paragraph (1), anyone may provide the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries with information with an intent that the relevant plant variety is not entitled to protection for a violation of Article 16, 21 or 22, together with evidence. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Matters to be published in the Official Gazette when applications are laid-open pursuant to paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 38 (Right of provisional protection) (1) An applicant for plant variety protection shall have an exclusive right to exploit the applied plant variety as a business from the date the application is laid-open.
  (2) Where any of the following applies to an application for plant variety protection after the application is laid-open, the rights under paragraph (1) shall be deemed not to have arisen ab initio:
  1. Where the application for plant variety protection is abandoned, withdrawn or invalidated;
  2. Where a decision to reject the application for plant variety protection becomes final and conclusive.
  (3) Where a person having the right under paragraph (1) exercises such right and any subparagraph of paragraph (2) applies to the application for plant variety protection, he or she shall be liable for compensation for damage caused by the exercise of his or her right to other parties.
  (4) Articles 83 through 89 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the right under paragraph (1).

Article 39 (Exercise of right of provisional protection and suspension of legal proceedings) (1) Where a lawsuit on an infringement of the right under Article 38(1) is lodged or an application for provisional attachment or provisional disposition is filed, the court may suspend legal proceedings by decision until a decision or trial ruling on the application for plant variety protection becomes final and conclusive upon request of the relevant party or ex officio, if deemed necessary to do so.
  (2) No one shall file an objection to a decision on the application under paragraph (1).
  (3) If the grounds for suspension under paragraph (1) cease to exist or other circumstances change, the court may cancel the decision under paragraph (1).

Article 40 (Examination of applied plant varieties) (1) Examiners shall examine whether applied plant varieties meet the requirements provided for in Articles 17 through 20.
  (2) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may entrust inspections or tests for examinations under paragraph (1) to research institutes, universities, or agencies or organizations deemed suitable for conducting inspections or tests. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Matters necessary for the methods of, standards and procedures for examinations under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 41 (Submission of data) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may require an applicant for plant variety protection to submit data, such as seed samples if necessary for examinations under Article 40(1). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Applicants ordered to submit data under paragraph (1) shall comply with the order unless good cause exists.

Article 42 (Decision of rejection and notification of grounds for rejection) (1) An examiner shall decide to reject an application for plant variety protection in any of the following cases (hereinafter referred to as "reason for rejection"):
  1. Where it is not entitled to plant variety protection for a violation of Article 4, 16, 21, 22, 25(1) or (2), 27(2) or (5), 28(1), 30(3) or 41(2);
  2. Where a person without rights files an application;
  3. Violation of any relevant treaty, etc.;
  (2) Where an examiner makes a decision of rejection pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall, in advance, notify the applicant for plant variety protection of the grounds for such rejection, and give the applicant an opportunity to submit a written opinion within a prescribed period.
  (3) If a decision of rejection is made under paragraph (1), the examiner shall serve a certified copy of the decision of rejection on the applicant for plant variety protection, and publish the decision of rejection in the Official Gazette.
  (4) Matters to be published in the Official Gazette regarding a decision of rejection under paragraph (3) and other matters shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 43 (Decision of plant variety protection) (1) If no grounds for rejecting an application for plant variety protection are found, an examiner shall make a decision of plant variety protection.
  (2) The decision of plant variety protection under paragraph (1) shall be made in writing and the grounds therefor shall be stated.
  (3) Where a decision of plant variety protection is made under paragraph (1), the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall serve a certified copy of the decision of plant variety protection on the applicant for plant variety protection and publish the decision of plant variety protection in the Official Gazette. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Matters to be published in the Official Gazette regarding a decision of plant variety protection under paragraph (3) and other matters shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 44 (Suspension of examination or legal proceedings) (1) Procedures for the examination of an application for plant variety protection may be suspended until a trial ruling becomes final and conclusive or legal proceedings are completed if necessary for examining the application for plant variety protection.
  (2) The court may suspend legal proceedings until a decision becomes final and conclusive, if necessary in relation to the lawsuit.

Article 45 (Application mutatis mutandis of the Patent Act) Subparagraphs 1 through 5 and 7 of Article 148 of the Patent Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the examination of applications for plant variety protection.

SECTION 4 Plant Variety Protection Fees and Registration of Plant Variety Protection

Article 46 (Plant variety protection fees) (1) A person who intends to register the establishment of a plant variety right under Article 54(1) shall pay fees.
  (2) The plant variety right holder shall annually pay plant variety protection fees to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries during the duration of the plant variety right. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) An interested party in a plant variety right may pay plant variety protection fees regardless of the intent of a person required to pay such fees under paragraph (1) or (2).
  (4) Where an interested party in a plant variety right pays plant variety protection fees under paragraph (3), he or she may claim reimbursement of such fees to the extent that a person liable to pay such fees has enjoyed benefit therefrom.
  (5) Matters necessary in relation to the amount of plant variety protection fees, method of payment, and period for payment under paragraph (1) or (2) and other matters shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 47 (Payment of plant variety protection fees after expiration of period for payment) (1) A person who intends to register the establishment of a plant variety right or the plant variety right holder may pay plant variety protection fees within six months even after the expiration of the period for payment prescribed in Article 46(5). <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  (2) If plant variety protection fees are paid under paragraph (1), an amount determined by the joint ministerial decree, which shall not exceed two times the plant variety protection fees determined under Article 46(5), shall be paid. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) If plant variety protection fees are not paid within a prescribed period under paragraph (1), an application for plant variety protection shall be deemed abandoned by a person who intends to register the establishment of a plant variety right; the plant variety right of its holder shall be deemed lapsed, retroactively on the date following the expiration of the validity period for which the plant variety protection fees are paid under Article 46(1) or (2).
  [Title Amended on Feb. 11, 2020]

Article 48 (Settlement of outstanding plant variety protection fees) (1) Where a person who intends to register the establishment of a plant variety right or the plant variety right holder fails to pay some of the plant variety protection fees within the period specified under Article 46(5) or 47(1), the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall require him or her to settle the outstanding plant variety protection fees. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) A person receiving an order to settle outstanding plant variety protection fees under paragraph (1) may do so within one month after receipt of such order.
  (3) A person who settles outstanding plant variety protection fees pursuant to paragraph (2) shall pay an amount determined by the joint ministerial decree, which shall not exceed two times the amount payable, in any of the following cases: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 11, 2020>
  1. Where outstanding plant variety protection fees are settled after the expiration of the period for payment specified in Article 46(5);
  2. Where outstanding plant variety protection fees are settled after the expiration of the period for payment specified in Article 47(1) (hereinafter referred to as "additional period for payment").

Article 49 (Applications for plant variety protection and reinstatement of plant variety rights, by additional payment or settlement of outstanding plant variety protection fees) (1) Where a person who intends to register the establishment of a plant variety right or the plant variety right holder fails to pay plant variety protection fees within the additional period for payment or fails to settle outstanding fees within the period for settlement under Article 48(2) for grounds not attributable to him or her, he or she may pay the plant variety protection fees or settle outstanding fees within 14 days from the date such grounds cease to exist; provided, this shall not apply where six months have passed since the expiration date of the additional payment period or the expiration date of the settlement period, whichever comes later. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  (2) Notwithstanding Article 47(3), a person who pays plant variety protection fees or settles outstanding plant variety protection fees shall be deemed not to have abandoned his or her application for plant variety protection, and the plant variety right shall be deemed to have existed retroactively from the expiration of the period for payment of plant variety protection fees. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  (3) Where a plant variety right for a protected plant variety being exploited is extinguished for a failure to pay plant variety protection fees within the additional period for payment or to settle outstanding fees within the period for settlement under Article 48(2), the plant variety right holder may file an application for reinstatement of the extinguished right after paying an amount three times the plant variety protection fees under Article 46 within three months after the expiration of the additional period for payment or period for settlement. In such cases, the plant variety right shall be deemed to have existed retroactively from the expiration of the period for payment of plant variety protection fees. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  (4) No application for plant variety protection or plant variety right under paragraph (2) or (3) shall be effective to another person's exploitation of a protected plant variety during the following periods (hereafter referred to as "period of limitation of effect" in this Article): <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  1. A period from the date of expiration of the additional period for payment to the date payment is made;
  2. A period from the date of expiration of the additional period for payment to the date outstanding fees are settled.
  (5) A person who conducts or prepares to conduct the business of exploiting a plant variety for which an application for plant variety protection is filed under paragraph (2) or (3) in good faith in Korea or a plant variety right during the period of limitation of effect shall have the non-exclusive license of the plant variety right for which an application for plant variety protection is filed within the scope of purposes of the plant variety being exploited or within the scope of purposes of the business being prepared.
  (6) A person who has a non-exclusive license pursuant to paragraph (5) shall make reasonable payments to the relevant plant variety right holder or the relevant exclusive license holder.

Article 50 (Exemption from plant variety protection fees) Notwithstanding Article 46, plant variety protection fees shall not be charged in any of the following cases: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Where the State or a local government is required to pay plant variety protection fees in order to register the establishment of a plant variety right;
  2. Where the State or a local government is required to pay plant variety protection fees during the duration of a plant variety right;
  3. Where a beneficiary as defined in Article 5 of the National Basic Living Security Act is required to pay plant variety protection fees in order to register the establishment of a plant variety right;
  4. Other cases prescribed by the joint ministerial decree.

Article 51 (Refund of plant variety protection fees) Plant variety protection fees are refundable only if they are paid erroneously.

Article 52 (Plant variety register) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall keep a plant variety register and register the following matters: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Establishment, transfer and extinguishment of plant variety rights or limitation on the disposal thereof;
  2. Establishment, preservation, transfer, change, lapse, or disposal of exclusive licenses or non-exclusive licenses, or limitation on the disposal thereof;
  3. Establishment, transfer, change, lapse, or disposal of a pledge right on a plant variety right, exclusive license or non-exclusive license.
  (2) Except as provided in paragraph (1), matters to be registered, procedures of registration, and other matters necessary for registration shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may request any of the following materials or information from each of the following persons to perform registration work specified in paragraph (1) and (2). In such cases, the person so requested shall comply unless there is a compelling reason not to do so: <Added on Jul. 20, 2015; on Jul. 26, 2017>
  1. A certified copy or abstract of a resident registration card: The Minister of the Interior and Safety;
  2. Computerized information data on registered matters regarding family relations under the Act on the Registration of Family Relationships: The Minister of the National Court Administration.

Article 53 (Official Gazette on plant variety protection) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall publish an Official Gazette on plant variety protection on a monthly basis. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

SECTION 5 Plant Variety Rights

Article 54 (Registration of establishment of plant variety rights) (1) A plant variety right shall come into existence by completing the registration of establishment pursuant to Article 52(1)1.
  (2) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall register the establishment of a plant variety right in any of the following cases: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 11, 2020>
  1. Payment of plant variety protection fees under Article 46(1);
  2. Payment of plant variety protection fees after the expiration of the period of payment under Article 47(1);
  3. Settlement of outstanding plant variety protection fees under Article 48(2);
  4. Payment of plant variety protection fees or settlement of outstanding fees under Article 49(1);
  5. Exemption from plant variety protection fees under Article 50.
  (3) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall keep and manage a specified quantity of samples of seeds of a plant variety, the plant variety right of which is established and registered pursuant to paragraph (2), as determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries. In such cases, where the samples of seeds are saplings, trophosomes or aquatic plants, the timing for, and methods of submission, other matters shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries has registered the establishment of a plant variety right pursuant to paragraph (2), he or she shall publish the following matters in the Official Gazette: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Name and address of the holder of the plant variety right (in the case of a corporation, its name, name of its representative or location of its place of business);
  2. Registration number of the plant variety right;
  3. Year, month and date of the registration of establishment of the plant variety right;
  4. Duration of the plant variety right.
  (5) When the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries has registered the establishment of a plant variety right pursuant to paragraph (2), he or she shall, without delay, issue a certificate of registration of a plant variety right prescribed by the joint ministerial decree to the plant variety right holder. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 55 (Duration of plant variety rights) The duration of a plant variety right shall be 20 years from the date the establishment of the plant variety right is registered; provided, the duration of a plant variety right for fruit trees and standing trees shall be 25 years.

Article 56 (Effect of plant variety rights) (1) The plant variety right holder shall have an exclusive right to exploit the protected plant variety as a business; provided, where an exclusive license is established for the plant variety right, this shall not apply to the extent that the exclusive license holder has an exclusive right to exploit the protected plant variety pursuant to Article 61(2).
  (2) In addition to the right granted under paragraph (1), the plant variety right holder shall have an exclusive right to exploit, as a business, harvested material from the seeds of a protected plant variety using the misappropriated seeds without permission of the plant variety right holder, or to exploit products directly produced from such harvested material; provided, this shall not apply to a product directly manufactured by a person who has no knowledge that he or she does not have a legitimate right thereto.
  (3) The effect of a plant variety right granted under paragraph (1) or (2) shall also apply to the following plant varieties:
  1. Any plant variety essentially derived from a protected plant variety (limited to a protected plant variety not essentially derived from other plant varieties);
  2. Any plant variety not clearly distinguishable from a protected plant variety under Article 18;
  3. Any plant variety, the seeds of which require the repeated use of a protected plant variety.
  (4) For purposes of paragraph (3)1, a plant variety that is derived from an initial plant variety or existing derived plant variety, while retaining the expression of the essential characteristics that result from the genotype or combination of genotypes of the initial plant variety and is clearly distinguishable from the initial plant variety, but the essential characteristics of which conform to the initial plant variety in the expression of the essential characteristics, except for differences in characteristics that result from the act of derivation, shall be deemed a derived plant variety.

Article 57 (Limitation on effect of plant variety rights) (1) A plant variety right under Article 56 shall not be effective in any of the following cases:
  1. Exploitation of a protected plant variety for private consumption for non-profit purposes;
  2. Exploitation of a protected plant variety for experimental or research purposes;
  3. Exploitation of a protected plant variety for breeding other plant varieties.
  (2) Where a farmer or fisher saves seeds to re-sow them for production, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may limit the right to such plant variety. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The scope, procedures and methods of limitation under paragraph (2) and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 58 (Restriction on effect of plant variety rights) No plant variety right under Article 56 shall be effective to the seeds of a protected plant variety sold or circulated in the Republic of Korea, harvested material therefrom and products directly produced from such harvested material by the holder of the plant variety right, exclusive license or non-exclusive license except for any of the following:
  1. Propagating the seeds of a protected plant variety using the seeds of the protected plant variety sold or circulated in the Republic of Korea, harvested material therefrom and products directly produced from such harvested material;
  2. Exporting the seeds of a protected plant variety, harvested material therefrom and products directly produced from such harvested material for propagation.

Article 59 (Prohibition against restriction on plant variety rights) The Government shall not impose any restriction on the exploitation of plant variety rights except as provided in this Act.

Article 60 (Transfer of plant variety rights) (1) Plant variety rights may be transferred.
  (2) Where a plant variety right is jointly owned, none of the joint owners shall perform any of the following acts without the consent of the other owners:
  1. Transferring co-owned share or establishing a pledge right to co-owned share;
  2. Establishing an exclusive license or granting a non-exclusive license for the relevant plant variety right.
  (3) Where a plant variety right is jointly owned, each owner may exploit the relevant protected plant variety without the consent of the other owners unless otherwise specifically provided by contract.

Article 61 (Exclusive licenses) (1) A plant variety right holder may establish an exclusive license for the plant variety right for any third person.
  (2) The holder of an exclusive license established under paragraph (1) shall have the exclusive right to exploit the relevant protected plant variety as a business within the extent specified by such establishment.
  (3) The holder of an exclusive license shall not transfer the exclusive license without the consent of the plant variety right holder except in any of the following cases:
  1. Where it is transferred together with the exploitation business;
  2. Inheritance;
  3. Other general succession.
  (4) The holder of an exclusive license shall not establish a pledge right to the exclusive license or grant a non-exclusive license without the consent of the plant variety right holder.
  (5) Article 60(2) and (3) shall apply mutatis mutandis to exclusive licenses.

Article 62 (Effect of registration of plant variety rights and exclusive licenses) (1) None of the following matters shall take effect unless entered in the plant variety register under Article 52:
  1. Extinguishment of a plant variety right following transfer (excluding inheritance or other general succession; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) or abandonment thereof or limitation on the disposal thereof;
  2. Establishment, transfer, change, extinguishment of an exclusive license or limitation on the disposal thereof;
  3. Establishment, transfer, extinguishment of a pledge right to a plant variety right or exclusive license, or limitation on the disposal thereof.
  (2) A person who inherits a plant variety right, exclusive license or pledge right or a general successor shall report on the intent to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries within 30 days from the date the relevant ground arises, as prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 63 (Non-exclusive licenses) (1) A plant variety right holder may grant a non-exclusive license for the plant variety right to any third person.
  (2) The holder of a non-exclusive license granted under paragraph (1) shall be entitled to exploit the relevant protected plant variety as a business, as prescribed by this Act, or within the extent specified by such granting.
  (3) A non-exclusive license granted under Article 67 may be transferred only if transferred together with the exploitation business.
  (4) A non-exclusive license, other than non-exclusive licenses granted under Article 67, shall not be transferred without the consent of the plant variety right holder (referring to the plant variety right holder and the exclusive license holder in the case of a non-exclusive license with regard to an exclusive license) except for transfer together with the exploitation business, inheritance or general succession.
  (5) No pledge right shall be established with respect to a non-exclusive license, other than non-exclusive licenses granted under Article 67, without the consent of the plant variety right holder (referring to the plant variety right holder and the exclusive license holder in the case of a non-exclusive license with regard to an exclusive license).
  (6) Article 60(2) and (3) shall apply mutatis mutandis to non-exclusive licenses.

Article 64 (Non-exclusive licenses based on prior use) A person who breeds a protected plant variety, for which an application for plant variety protection is filed as at the time an application for the plant variety protection is filed, without prior knowledge of such protected plant variety, or a person who has learned it from the breeder and conducts, or prepares to conduct, the business of exploiting the protected plant variety in the Republic of Korea shall be granted a non-exclusive license for the plant variety right, for which an application is filed, within the scope of the purpose of such business.

Article 65 (Non-exclusive licenses arising from exploitation prior to registration of requests for invalidation adjudication) (1) Where any of the following persons conducts or prepares to conduct the business of exploiting a protected plant variety in the Republic of Korea, not knowing the relevant plant variety right falls under the grounds for invalidation before the registration of a request for invalidation adjudication for such plant variety right, he or she shall have a non-exclusive license for the plant variety right or exclusive license existing as at the time the plant variety right is invalidated within the scope of the purpose of such business:
  1. The initial plant variety right holder where one of the plant variety rights to the same plant variety is invalidated;
  2. The initial plant variety right holder where a plant variety right is invalidated and a plant variety right is granted to the legitimate person for the same plant variety;
  3. A person who has obtained and registered an exclusive license, non-exclusive license or non-exclusive license for such exclusive license as at the time of filing a request for invalidation adjudication on the invalidated plant variety right in cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 2; provided, no registration is required where Article 74(2) is applicable.
  (2) A person who acquires a non-exclusive license under paragraph (1) shall make a reasonable payment to the holder of the relevant plant variety right or exclusive license.

Article 66 (Non-exclusive licenses following transfer of plant variety right due to exercise of pledge right) Where a plant variety right holder conducts the business of exploiting the relevant protected plant variety before a pledge right is established for the plant variety right, he or she shall have a non-exclusive license for the plant variety right even if the plant variety right is transferred by auction, etc. In such cases, the plant variety right holder shall make a reasonable payment to the person to whom the plant variety right is transferred by auction, etc.

Article 67 (Adjudication on establishment of non-exclusive licenses) (1) Where any of the following applies to a protected plant variety, a person who intends to exploit the protected plant variety may file with the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries a request for adjudication on the establishment of a non-exclusive license (hereinafter referred to as "adjudication"); provided, a request for adjudication under subparagraph 1 or 2 may be made only where it is impossible to have consultation on granting a non-exclusive license with the plant variety right holder or the exclusive license holder for the relevant protected plant variety or where no agreement is reached upon such consultation: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Where the protected plant variety has not been exploited in the Republic of Korea for at least three years without any natural disaster or a force majeure event or without good cause prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  2. Where the protected plant variety has not been exploited in the Republic of Korea on a substantial business scale for at least three consecutive years without good cause or fails to meet domestic demand to a reasonable extent and under reasonable conditions;
  3. Where it is necessary to non-commercially exploit the protected plant variety for urgent adjustment of supply and demand or propagation due to a war, natural disaster or calamity;
  4. Where it is necessary to exploit the protected plant variety in order to correct matters recognized as unfair trade practices according to judicial or administrative proceedings.
  (2) Paragraph (1) shall not apply to a protected plant variety for which three years have not passed from the date of registration of the establishment of the plant variety right. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  (3) When conducting an adjudication, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall examine whether the establishment of non-exclusive license is necessary on an application-by-application basis. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) When conducting an adjudication, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall attach a condition that the non-exclusive license shall be executed primarily for the supply to meet the domestic demand; provided, this shall not apply where an adjudication is conducted for a request made under paragraph (1)4. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) When conducting an adjudication under paragraph (1)4, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may take into consideration the intent that the adjudication is to correct unfair trade practices. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (6) When conducting an adjudication, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall submit the relevant case for deliberation by the Seed Commission under Article 118. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 68 (Service of requests for adjudication) Upon receipt of a request for adjudication under Article 67(1), the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall serve a copy of the request on the plant variety right holder or the exclusive license holder relating to the request or a person with a registered right concerning the relevant plant variety right, and shall give such person an opportunity to submit a written reply or opinion within a specified period. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 69 (Methods of adjudication) (1) An adjudication shall be rendered in writing and the grounds therefor shall be stated.
  (2) An adjudication rendered under paragraph (1) shall clearly state the following matters:
  1. The scope and term of the non-exclusive license;
  2. The consideration for the license, and method and timing for payment thereof.
  (3) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries receives a request for an extension of the duration of a non-exclusive license under paragraph (2)1, he or she shall not reject the request if grounds for establishing an existing non-exclusive license still exist. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 70 (Service of certified copy of written adjudication) (1) When an adjudication has been made, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall serve a certified copy of the written adjudication on the parties thereto. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) When a certified copy of the written adjudication is served on the parties thereto under paragraph (1), an agreement between the parties shall be deemed reached as stated in the written adjudication.

Article 71 (Deposit of consideration) A person required to pay a consideration under Article 69(2)2 shall deposit the consideration in any of the following cases:
  1. Where the person to receive the consideration refuses to, or unable to, receive it;
  2. Where a lawsuit under Article 104(1) is instituted against the consideration;
  3. Where a pledge right is established on the relevant plant variety right or exclusive license; provided, this shall not apply where consent is obtained from the pledgee.

Article 72 (Invalidation of adjudication) (1) If a person granted an adjudication under Article 69(1) fails to pay or deposit a consideration (referring to the first payment where the consideration is paid periodically or in installments) within the period of payment set under Article 69(2)2, the adjudication shall be invalidated.
  (2) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may revoke adjudication at the request of any of the interested parties or ex officio in any of the following cases: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Where a person granted an adjudication fails to exploit the non-exclusive license;
  2. Where the grounds for an adjudication on the establishment of the non-exclusive license right cease to exist and are unlikely to recur;
  3. Where a person granted an adjudication and required to pay the consideration periodically or in installments fails to make payments after the first installment payment or fails to deposit the consideration.
  (3) Articles 67(6), 68, 69(1) and 70(1) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the revocation under paragraph (2).
  (4) If an adjudication is revoked under paragraph (2), the relevant non-exclusive license shall be extinguished from the time of such revocation.

Article 73 (Restriction on grounds for objections to adjudication) Where an administrative appeal is filed under Article 3(1) of the Administrative Appeals Act or litigation for revocation is filed under the Administrative Litigation Act with respect to adjudication, the consideration determined by the adjudication shall not serve as the grounds for objection to such adjudication.

Article 74 (Effect of registration of non-exclusive licenses) (1) If a non-exclusive license is registered, it shall also be effective to a person who acquires the plant variety right or exclusive license after the registration.
  (2) A non-exclusive license granted under Articles 49(5), 64 through 66 and 102 shall take effect under paragraph (1) even if the license is not registered.
  (3) The transfer, change or extinguishment of a non-exclusive license or the establishment, transfer, change or extinguishment of a pledge right to a non-exclusive license or limitation on the disposal thereof shall not be asserted against a third party unless registered.

Article 75 (Restriction on abandoning plant variety rights) (1) A plant variety right holder shall not abandon the plant variety right without the consent of the exclusive license holder, pledgee or non-exclusive license holder under Article 61(4) or 63(1).
  (2) An exclusive license holder shall not abandon the exclusive license without the consent of the pledgee or non-exclusive license holder under Article 61(4).
  (3) A non-exclusive license holder shall not abandon the non-exclusive license without the consent of the pledgee.

Article 76 (Effect of abandonment) If a plant variety right, exclusive license or non-exclusive license is abandoned, the plant variety right, exclusive license or non-exclusive license shall be extinguished from the time of such abandonment.

Article 77 (Pledge rights) Where a pledge right is established on a plant variety right, exclusive license or non-exclusive license, the pledgee shall not exploit the relevant plant variety unless otherwise specifically stipulated by a contract.

Article 78 (Subrogation of pledge rights) A pledge right may also be exercised for any consideration or goods to be received for the exploitation of a protected plant variety. In such cases, the seizure shall be made before the payment or delivery thereof.

Article 79 (Revocation of plant variety rights) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may revoke a plant variety right in any of the following cases; provided, where subparagraph 2 is applicable, he or she shall revoke such plant variety right: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Where the requirements provided for in Article 19 or 20 cannot be met;
  2. Where the duty to maintain a protected plant variety under Article 82 is not fulfilled;
  3. Where the denomination of a plant variety registered under Article 117(1) is revoked.
  (2) If a plant variety right is revoked under paragraph (1), the plant variety right shall be extinguished from the time of suchrevocation.
  (3) Article 42(2) through (4) shall apply mutatis mutandis to revocation under paragraph (1). In such cases, "decision of refusal" shall be construed as "revocation."

Article 80 (Extinguishment of plant variety rights in the absence of heir) Where inheritance commences and no heir exists, the plant variety right shall be extinguished.

Article 81 (Reporting on exploitation of plant variety rights) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may require plant variety right holders, exclusive license holders or non-exclusive license holders to report on whether the relevant protected plant variety are exploited, the scale thereof, etc. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 82 (Duty to maintain protected plant varieties) (1) The plant variety right holder shall ensure that the essential characteristics of the protected plant variety as at the registration of establishment of the plant variety right are maintained for the duration of the plant variety right.
  (2) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may order the plant variety right holder to submit data, such as seed samples necessary for testing and verifying whether the essential characteristics of the protected plant variety are maintained under paragraph (1). In such cases, the plant variety right holder so ordered shall comply unless there is a compelling reason not to do so. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

SECTION 6 Protection of Plant Variety Right Holders

Article 83 (Right to request prohibition against infringement of right) (1) Where a person infringes, or is likely to infringe, his or her right, the plant variety right holder or the exclusive license holder may file a request for prohibition on or prevention of such infringement.
  (2) When filing a request under paragraph (1), the plant variety right holder or the exclusive license holder may request the destruction of the article that has caused an infringement, removal of equipment provided for such infringement, and other acts necessary for preventing an infringement.

Article 84 (Acts deemed infringements) Any of the following acts shall be deemed an infringement of a plant variety right or an exclusive license:
  1. Exploiting the protected plant variety of another person as a business without permission of the plant variety right holder or the exclusive license holder;
  2. Using the denomination of a plant variety, the same as, or similar to, that of the plant variety of another person for a plant variety of the genus or species to which the protected plant variety belongs.

Article 85 (Right to claim damages) (1) The plant variety right holder or the exclusive license holder may claim damages against a person who infringes his or her right either intentionally or by negligence.
  (2) Articles 128 through 132 of the Patent Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to claims for damages under paragraph (1).

Article 86 (Presumption of negligence) A person who infringes the plant variety right or exclusive license of another person shall be presumed negligent in respect of such infringement.

Article 87 (Credit recovery for plant variety right holders) Upon the request of a plant variety right holder or an exclusive license holder, the court may order a person who has impaired the business credit of the plant variety right holder or exclusive license holder intentionally or by negligence by infringing the plant variety right or exclusive license of such person to take measures necessary for the recovery of the business credit of the plant variety right holder or the exclusive license holder, in lieu of claim of damages or in addition thereto.

Article 88 (Labeling of protected plant varieties) The plant variety right holder, exclusive license holder or non-exclusive license holder may label the relevant plant variety as a protected plant variety.

Article 89 (Prohibition against false labeling) No person shall commit any of the following acts: <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  1. Affixing a label on the container or package of the seeds of a plant variety, which is not protected or for which an application for plant variety protection is not filed, as if it were protected or an application for plant variety protection were filed, or affixing a misleading label thereon;
  2. Indicating in business advertisements, labels, business documents, etc. a plant variety, which is not protected or for which an application for plant variety protection is not filed, as if it were protected or an application for plant variety protection were filed.

SECTION 7 Adjudication

Article 90 (Plant Variety Protection Tribunal) (1) A Plant Variety Protection Tribunal (hereinafter referred to as the "Tribunal") shall be established within the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs to take charge of adjudication and retrial on plant variety protection. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The Tribunal shall consist of up to eight plant variety protection adjudicators (hereinafter referred to as "adjudicator"), including one chairperson; one of the adjudicators, other than the chairperson, shall be a full-time adjudicator.
  (3) Except as provided in paragraph (2), the composition and operation of the Tribunal and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 91 (Adjudication on decision of rejection or decision of revocation) Where a person who is given a decision of rejection under Article 42(1) or a decision of revocation under Article 79 is dissatisfied with such decision, he or she may request an adjudication within 30 days from the date of receipt of a certified copy of such decision.

Article 92 (Invalidation adjudication for plant variety protection) (1) Where any of the following applies to plant variety protection, a person interested in the plant variety protection or examiner may request adjudication of invalidation:
  1. Violation of Article 16, 21, 22, 25(1) or (2), 28(1) or 30(3); provided, where a request for an adjudication of invalidation is to be filed on the grounds of a failure to meet the uniformity or stability requirements under subparagraph 3 or 4 of Article 16, such request may be filed only where uniformity or stability has been examined based on the documents submitted by the applicant;
  2. Plant variety protection for a person without rights;
  3. Violation of any relevant treaty, etc.;
  4. Where the plant variety right holder becomes ineligible for the plant variety right under Article 22 or such plant variety protection is in violation of a treaty, etc. after the plant variety has been protected.
  (2) An adjudication under paragraph (1) may be requested whenever benefits are expected therefrom.
  (3) Where a trial ruling to invalidate a plant variety right becomes final and conclusive, the plant variety right shall be deemed not to have existed ab initio; provided, where a trial ruling to invalidate the plant variety right becomes final and conclusive on the grounds provided in paragraph (1)4, the plant variety right shall be deemed not to have existed from the time it falls under that subparagraph.
  (4) Upon receipt of a request for adjudication made under paragraph (1), a chief adjudicator shall notify the plant variety right holder and exclusive license holder for the relevant plant variety, and other persons having a registered right to the plant variety protection of the intent thereof.

Article 93 (Method of requesting adjudication) (1) A person who intends to request adjudication shall include the following matters in a request for adjudication prescribed by the joint ministerial decree and submit it to the chairperson of the Tribunal: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Names and addresses of interested parties and agents (in the case of a corporation, its title and name of its representative and the location of its place of business);
  2. A denomination of the relevant plant variety;
  3. Filing date of an application for plant variety protection and serial number of the application for plant variety protection;
  4. Date of a decision of rejection, date of a decision of plant variety protection or date of a decision of revocation by an examiner;
  5. The intent of and grounds for filing a request.
  (2) Where a request for adjudication submitted under paragraph (1) is amended, the intent thereof shall not be amended; provided, this shall not apply to the grounds for a request specified in paragraph (1)5.

Article 94 (Adjudicators) (1) Upon receipt of a request for adjudication made under Article 93(1), the chairperson of the Tribunal shall require adjudicators to adjudicate the case.
  (2) As a matter of duty, each member of the Adjudication Committee shall adjudicate independently.
  (3) Qualifications for adjudicators shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 95 (Designation of adjudicators) (1) The chairperson of the Tribunal shall designate adjudicators to form a panel under Article 96 for each adjudication case.
  (2) The chairperson of the Tribunal shall designate a chief adjudicator from among adjudicators designated under paragraph (1), and the chief adjudicator shall have overall management of the adjudication case.
  (3) An adjudicator shall be excluded from deliberation and decisions on an adjudication case in any of the following cases:
  1. Where the adjudicator or his or her current or former spouse is a party to the adjudication case or is a joint right holder or a joint obligator with respect to the case;
  2. Where the adjudicator is a current or former relative of a party to the case;
  3. Where the adjudicator has given testimony, made an appraisal or given legal advice concerning the case;
  4. Where the adjudicator engages in or has engaged in the case as the agent of a party thereto;
  5. Where the adjudicator engages in or has engaged in the case as a legal representative of a party thereto;
  6. Where the adjudicator has a direct interest in the case.
  (4) Where circumstances indicate that it would be impracticable to expect a fair deliberation and decision from an adjudicator, a party may file a request for challenge to the adjudicator with the Tribunal, and the Tribunal shall decide to grant it if the request for challenge is deemed reasonable.
  (5) Where paragraph (3) or (4) applies to an adjudicator, he or she may recuse himself or herself from an adjudication case with the permission of the chairperson of the Tribunal.

Article 96 (Panel of adjudicators) (1) An adjudication shall be made by a panel of adjudicators consisting of three adjudicators.
  (2) The panel of adjudicators referred to in paragraph (1) shall reach an agreement by a majority vote.
  (3) The agreement reached shall not be disclosed.

Article 97 (Application mutatis mutandis of provisions of examination to adjudication on decision of rejection) `Articles 33, 35, 42(2) and 43 shall apply mutatis mutandis to an adjudication on a decision of rejection under Article 91.

Article 98 (Application mutatis mutandis of the Patent Act) (1) Articles 139, 141, 142, 147, 149, 151, 152(2) through (4), 153, 154(1) and (3) through (7), 155 through 160, 161(1) and (3), 162 through 166, 171, 172 and 176 of the Patent Act and Articles 143, 259, 299 and 367 of the Civil Procedure Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to adjudication under Articles 91 and 92.
  (2) For purposes of paragraph (1), "invalidation trial referred to in Articles 133(1), 134(1) and (2), or 137(1), or trial to confirm the scope of a patent right under Article 135(1) and (2)" in Article 139(1) of the Patent Act shall be construed as "adjudication of invalidation under Article 92(1)." <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016>
  (3) For purposes of paragraph (1), "Article 140(1), (3) through (5) or 140-2(1)" in Article 141(1)1 of the Patent Act shall be construed as "Article 93(1)," and "Article 82" in subparagraph 2b of that paragraph as "Article 125", respectively. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  (4) For purposes of paragraph (1), "Articles 133(1), 134(1) and (2), 135 and 137(1)" in Article 165(1) of the Patent Act shall be construed as "Article 92(1)," and "Article 132-17, 136 or 138" in paragraph (3) of that Article as "Article 91" and "patent attorney" in paragraph (7) of that Article as "person", respectively. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016>
  (2) Articles 187, 188 and 189 of the Patent Act shall apply to mutatis mutandis to lawsuits concerning the protection of plant varieties.
  (6) For purposes of paragraph (1), "Article 132-17" in Article 176(1) of the Patent Act shall be construed as "Article 91." <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016>

SECTION 8 Retrial and Lawsuits

Article 99 (Requests for retrial) (1) Parties may request the retrial of a final and conclusive decision.
  (2) Articles 451 through 453 of the Civil Procedure Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to requests for retrial under paragraph (1).

Article 100 (Request for appeal against decision by deception) (1) Where parties to a trial colluded to deceive the court into rendering a decision which leads to the infringement of any right or interest of a third party, the third party may request retrial for the final and conclusive decision (hereinafter referred to as "trial ruling by deception").
  (2) In making a request for retrial under paragraph (1), parties to adjudication shall be the joint appellees of retrial.

Article 101 (Restriction on effects of plant variety right reinstated by retrial) In any of the following cases, a plant variety right shall not be effective to an act performed in good faith before a request for retrial is registered but after the relevant decision becomes final and conclusive:
  1. Where the plant variety right is reinstated by retrial after having been invalidated;
  2. Where the establishment of a plant variety right is registered by retrial for an application for plant variety protection, for which a decision not to accept a request for appeal against a decision of refusal has been made.

Article 102 (Non-exclusive licenses for prior users of plant variety rights reinstated through retrial) Where any subparagraph of Article 101 is applicable, a person who conducts or prepares to conduct the business of exploiting a protected plant variety in the Republic of Korea in good faith after the relevant decision becomes final and conclusive but before a request for retrial is registered shall have a non-exclusive license within the scope of the purpose of such business.

Article 103 (Lawsuits against trial rulings) (1) The Patent Court of the Republic of Korea shall have exclusive jurisdiction over lawsuits on trial rulings and lawsuits on decisions to reject amendments to a written request for a trial or retrial.
  (2) A lawsuit prescribed in paragraph (1) shall be lodged only by a party, a participant, or a person whose application for participation in the relevant decision or retrial is filed but rejected.
  (3) A lawsuit prescribed in paragraph (1) shall be lodged within 30 days from the date of receipt of a certified copy of a trial ruling or decision.
  (4) The period referred to in paragraph (3) shall be unchangeable.
  (5) No lawsuit concerning matters on which a trial may be requested shall be brought unless such matters relate to a trial ruling.
  (6) No lawsuit under paragraph (1) shall be lodged independently on a ruling or decision on trial costs under Article 165 of the Patent Act applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 98.
  (7) An appeal against a ruling of the Patent Court may be lodged to the Supreme Court.

Article 104 (Lawsuit against decision on consideration) (1) A person dissatisfied with a decision on consideration under Article 69(2)2 may file a lawsuit with the court.
  (2) A lawsuit under paragraph (1) shall be lodged within 30 days from the date the relevant person is served a certified copy of the written adjudication.
  (3) In a lawsuit lodged under paragraph (1), the plant variety right holder, an exclusive license holder or non-exclusive license holder shall be the defendant.

Article 105 (Application mutatis mutandis of Patent Act) (1) Articles 180 and 184 of the Patent Act and Article 459(1) of the Civil Procedure Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to procedures and requests for retrial concerning the protection of plant varieties.
  (2) Articles 187, 188 and 189 of the Patent Act shall apply to mutatis mutandis to lawsuits concerning the protection of plant varieties.
  (3) For purposes of paragraph (2), "the Minister of Intellectual Property" in the first sentence of Article 187 of the Patent Act shall be construed as "the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries"; "Articles 133(1), 134(1) and (2), 135(1) and (2), 137(1), or 138(1) and (3)" in the proviso of that Article as "Article 92(1)"; and "Article 186(1)" in Article 189(1) of that Act as "Article 103(1)", respectively. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 29, 2016; Oct. 1, 2025>

CHAPTER III DENOMINATIONS OF PLANT VARIETIES

Article 106 (Denominations of plant varieties) (1) A plant variety for which an application is filed for protection under Article 30(1) shall have an identifiable denomination.
  (2) Where the denomination of a plant variety is registered or an application for registering the denomination of a plant variety is pending in the Republic of Korea or a foreign country, such denomination of the plant variety shall be used.

Article 107 (Requirements for registration of denominations of plant varieties) The denomination of a plant variety is ineligible for registration under Article 109(8), if:
  1. It consists of numbers only or includes symbols;
  2. It is indicated only with the relevant plant variety or the quality, quantity, time of production, method of production, method of use or time of use of the harvested material from the relevant pant variety;
  3. It is likely to mislead or cause confusion as it is the same as or similar to the denomination of another plant variety of the genus or species to which such plant variety belongs;
  4. It is likely to mislead or cause confusion as if the relevant plant variety were derived from or related to another plant variety, differently from the fact;
  5. It uses the name of a plant, genus or species, or is likely to be misled as, or confused with the name of a plant, genus or species;
  6. It falsely indicates a relationship with, or is likely to disparage or insult a country, race, ethnic group, gender, persons with disabilities, public organization, religion or deceased person;
  7. It includes the name, title or abbreviation of a renowned person; provided, this shall not apply where consent is obtained from such person;
  8. It is likely to mislead or confuse as to the origin of the plant variety or includes a misleading or confusing geographical indication;
  9. It is likely to be misled as, or confused with the same or a similar trademark in the process of registration, or registered under the Trademark Act before the filing date of an application for the denomination of the plant variety;
  10. The denomination itself or its meaning, etc. is likely to damage ordinary moral sense, good morals or public order.

Article 108 (Earlier applications for denominations of plant varieties) (1) Where two or more applications is filed to register the same denomination of a plant variety on different days, the applicant who first files an application for registration shall be only entitled to register the denomination.
  (2) Article 25(2) and (5) shall apply mutatis mutandis to registration of the denominations of plant varieties under paragraph (1). In such cases, "plant variety" shall be construed as "denomination of a plant variety", and "plant variety protection" as "registration of the denomination of a plant variety", respectively.

Article 109 (Procedures for registering denominations of plant varieties) (1) A person who intends to register the denomination of a plant variety (hereinafter referred to as "applicant to register the denomination of a plant variety") shall file an application to register the denomination of a plant variety with the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries with documents, etc. prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) If an application for plant variety protection is filed with the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries in cases falling under Article 106(1), an application to register the denomination of a plant variety shall be deemed filed. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) An examiner shall examine whether the denomination of a plant variety filed under paragraph (1) meets the requirements for registration of the denominations of plant varieties under Article 107.
  (4) Where any of the following applies to the denomination of a plant variety filed for registration, an examiner shall decide to reject the application for registration:
  1. Where a decision to reject the application for plant variety protection is made under Article 42(1);
  2. Where it violates Article 106;
  3. Where any subparagraph of Article 107 is applicable;
  4. Where the denomination of a plant variety is ineligible for registration under Article 108.
  (5) Where an examiner intends to reject an application to register the denomination of a plant variety pursuant to paragraph (4)2 through 4, he or she shall notify the applicant to register the denomination of the plant variety, of the grounds for rejection, and allow the applicant to submit a new denomination of the plant variety within 30 days from the date of notification.
  (6) Where none of the subparagraphs of paragraph (4) applies to an application to register the denomination of a plant variety under paragraph (1), an examiner shall publicly announce the application to register the denomination of a plant variety by publishing the same in the Official Gazette.
  (7) Where an application to register the denomination of a plant variety is publicly announced under paragraph (6), anyone may file an objection to registration of the denomination with the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries within 30 days from the date of public announcement. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (8) Where it is found that none of the subparagraphs of paragraph (4) applies to an application to register the denomination of a plant variety after the application to register the denomination of the plant variety pursuant to paragraph (6) is publicly announced or procedures for filing objections to registration of the denomination of the plant variety are completed, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall, without delay, register the denomination of the plant variety in the register of denominations of plant varieties and shall notify the applicant to register the denomination of the plant variety of such fact. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 110 (Filing objections to registration of denominations of plant varieties) When filing an objection to registration of the denomination of a plant variety, the applicant shall submit the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries a written objection to registration of the denomination of the plant variety, stating the grounds for objection, accompanied by necessary evidentiary documents. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 111 (Amendment of grounds for objection to registration of denominations of plant varieties) A person who files an objection to registration of the denomination of a plant variety (hereinafter referred to as "objector to registration of the denomination of a plant variety") may amend the grounds stated in the written objection against registration of the denomination of a plant variety or evidentiary documents within 30 days after the expiration of the period for filing objections against registration of the denomination of a plant variety. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>

Article 112 (Decisions on objections to registration of denominations of plant varieties) (1) Upon receipt of an objection to registration of the denomination of a plant variety, an examiner shall serve a copy of the written objection against registration of the denomination of a plant variety on the applicant for registration of the denomination of the plant variety and give him or her an opportunity to submit a written response within a specified period.
  (2) An examiner shall decide on the objection to registration of the denomination of a plant variety after the expiration of the period set under paragraph (1). <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  (3) A decision on an objection to registration of the denomination of a plant variety shall be made in writing, and grounds therefor shall be stated.
  (4) If a decision is made under paragraph (2), the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall serve a certified copy of the decision on the applicant for registration of the denomination of a plant variety and the objector to registration of the denomination of a plant variety. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) No objection shall be re-filed on the same grounds once a decision is made on an objection to registration of the denomination of a plant variety.

Article 113 (Ex officio decisions of rejection after public announcement of applications for registration of denominations of plant varieties) (1) Where an examiner finds that any of the subparagraphs of Article 109(4) applies to an application to register the denomination of a plant variety after the public announcement thereof, he or she may decide to reject the application ex officio.
  (2) Where a decision of rejection is made under paragraph (1), no decision shall be made on an objection to registration of the denomination of a plant variety even if an objection to registration of the denomination of a plant variety is filed.
  (3) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries makes a decision of rejection pursuant to paragraph (1) and an objection to registration of the denomination of a plant variety is filed, he or she shall serve a certified copy of the decision of rejection on the objector to registration of the denomination of a plant variety. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Article 42(2) through (4) shall apply mutatis mutandis to decisions of rejection made under paragraph (1). In such cases, "plant variety protection" shall be construed as "registration of the denomination of a plant variety."

Article 114 (Multiple objections to registration of denominations of plant varieties) (1) An examiner may merge or divide the examination of, or decision on, two or more objections to registration of the denomination of a plant variety.
  (2) Where two or more objections to registration of the denomination of a plant variety are filed, and where an examiner deems that one of the objections to registration of the denomination of a plant variety is reasonable upon examination, he or she may choose not to decide on the rest of the objections.
  (3) Where a decision of rejection is made as an objection to registration of the denomination of a plant variety is deemed reasonable pursuant to paragraph (2), the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall also serve a certified copy of the decision of rejection on the objector to registration of the denominations of a plant variety on which no decision is made. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 115 (Filing objections to decisions to reject registration of denominations of plant varieties) `Articles 110 through 114 shall apply mutatis mutandis to an objection to a decision to reject registration of the denomination of a plant variety.

Article 116 (Use of denominations of plant varieties) (1) No person shall sell, supply, export or import seeds misappropriating the denomination of a plant variety registered by another person under Article 109(8) (excluding a protected plant variety, the plant variety right of which is established and registered under Article 54(2)).
  (2) No person shall sell or supply seeds using the denomination of a plant variety unregistered in the register of the denominations of plant varieties under Article 109(8).
  (3) Where an applicant for registration of the denomination of a plant variety or a successor to the plant variety uses the denomination of a plant variety registered under Article 109(8), he or she may indicate a trademark together with the denomination. In such cases, the denomination of the plant variety shall be indicated in a readily identifiable manner.

Article 117 (Revocation of denominations of plant varieties) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall revoke the denomination of a plant variety registered under Article 109(8) in any of the following cases: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Where a reason falling under any of subparagraphs 2 through 4 of Article 109(4) is found;
  2. Where there is a ruling that prohibits the use of the denomination of a plant variety;
  3. Other cases prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries intends to revoke the denomination of a plant variety pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall notify the applicant for the denomination of the plant variety registered of the grounds for revocation and require him or her to submit a new denomination of the plant variety within 30 days from the date of notification. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Article 109(3) through (8) and Articles 110 through 114 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the new denominations of plant varieties submitted under paragraph (2).

Chapter IV Supplementary Provisions

Article 118 (Seed Commission) (1) An Agricultural Seed Commission or a Maritime Seed Commission (hereinafter referred to as the "Seed Commission") shall be established within the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries to: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Advise the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries on the protection of plant variety rights;
  2. Deliberate on adjudication on the establishment of non-exclusive licenses under Article 67;
  3. Mediate disputes on the infringements of plant variety rights.
  (2) The Seed Commission shall consist of one chairperson and at least 10 but up to 15 members (hereinafter referred to as "Seed Commission members"), including one full-time adjudicator of the Tribunal established under Article 90(2).
  (3) The Seed Commission members shall be appointed or entrusted by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries from among the following persons; and the chairperson shall be appointed or entrusted by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries from among the Seed Commission members: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. A current or former Grade III or higher grade public official (including a public official in the general service of the Senior Civil Service) who has experience in seed-related duties;
  2. A current or former associate professor or higher in a university under the Higher Education Act, and who has studied the seed-related field;
  3. A licensed attorney-at-law or patent attorney;
  4. A current or former executive officer of an organization relating to agriculture, forestry or fishery;
  5. A current or former executive officer of an association relating to the seed industry;
  6. Persons recommended by a civic organization (referring to a non-profit, non-governmental organization as defined in Article 2 of the Assistance for Non-Profit, Non-Governmental Organizations Act).
  (4) The term of office of the Seed Commission members shall be two years, which may be renewed for two further terms.
  (5) The composition and operation of the Seed Commission and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 119 (Mediation of disputes) (1) A person who intends to seek mediation of a dispute on the infringement of a plant variety right may file a request for mediation with the Seed Commission.
  (2) A person who intends to request mediation under paragraph (1) shall submit a request for mediation prescribed by the joint ministerial decree with the Seed Commission. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Upon receipt of a request for mediation filed under paragraph (2), the chairperson of the Seed Commission shall refer the case to the mediation panel under paragraph (4) if deemed necessary, and send a copy of the request for mediation to the other party to the dispute.
  (4) Upon receipt of a request for mediation filed under paragraph (1), the Seed Commission may form a mediation panel with three members and conduct mediation within one year after receipt of the request for mediation; provided, where a good cause exists including where a case test cultivation is necessary, it may extend the period of mediation, as prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 29, 2017>
  (5) The composition and operation of a mediation panel and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (6) A mediation panel may have a person who files a request for mediation of a dispute on an infringement of a plant variety right under paragraph (1) bear expenses incurred in relation to investigations; provided, the mediation panel may require parties to the case to equally bear expenses where mediation is successful and there is no special agreement thereon.
  (7) The method of calculation and payment of expenses under paragraph (6), period of payment, and other matters shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 120 (Exclusion of member) (1) A Seed Commission member shall be excluded from the relevant mediation in any of the following cases:
  1. Where either of the following persons becomes a party to the relevant dispute or is in a relationship in which a right or obligation is held jointly with a party to the relevant dispute:
  a. A Seed Commission member;
  b. A current or former spouse of a Seed Commission member;
  2. Where a Seed Commission member is a current or former relative of a party to the relevant dispute;
  3. Where a Seed Commission member gives testimony or makes an appraisal regarding the relevant dispute;
  4. Where a Seed Commission member engages or has engaged in the relevant dispute as the agent of a party to the dispute.
  (2) Where circumstances indicate that it would be impracticable to expect the fair execution of duties by a Seed Commission member, a party may file a request for challenge with the Seed Commission, which shall make a decision to accept such challenge if the request is deemed reasonable.
  (3) If paragraph (1) or (2) applies to a Seed Commission member, he or she may recuse himself or herself from mediation with the permission of the chairperson of the Seed Commission.

Article 121 (Requests for data) (1) The Seed Commission may request the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the heads of affiliated agencies to provide necessary cooperation, such as the submission of data or opinions, test cultivation and genetic testing, if deemed necessary for mediating disputes. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Upon receipt of a request for cooperation under paragraph (1), the heads of agencies shall cooperate therein unless any justifiable grounds exist.

Article 122 (Request for attendance) (1) The Seed Commission may request a party, his or her agent or interested parties to attend a meeting or submit relevant documents, if necessary.
  (2) Where the Seed Commission requests a party, his or her agent or interested parties to attend a meeting or submit necessary relevant documents under paragraph (1), it shall send a written request by no later than seven days before the scheduled date for the meeting.
  (3) The written request under paragraph (2) shall contain an intent that a failure to comply without justifiable grounds shall be deemed to be a waiver of an opportunity to make a statement.
  (4) Where a party fails to comply with a request for attendance or a request for submission of data under paragraph (1) without justifiable grounds, the case shall be deemed not mediated. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>

Article 123 (Ex officio mediation decision) (1) Where parties fail to reach an agreement or the applicant's claim is deemed reasonable, the Seed Commission may make an ex officio decision in lieu of mediation (hereinafter referred to as "ex officio mediation decision") mediation to the extent that it is not contrary to the purpose thereof, taking into consideration the interests of the parties and all other circumstances.
  (2) An ex officio mediation decision may include:
  1. Suspension of infringement;
  2. Compensation for damage or any other remedy;
  3. Measures necessary for preventing recurrence of the same or similar infringements.
  (3) The text and grounds therefor shall be stated in an ex officio mediation decision, and all mediators involved therein shall put signature and seal thereto; and the original copy shall be served on the relevant party without delay.
  (4) If a party fails to file an objection within 14 days from the date it is served a decision pursuant to paragraph (3), it shall be deemed to have accepted the ex officio mediation decision.
  (5) If an objection is filed within the period referred to in paragraph (4), the Seed Commission shall, without delay, notify the other party of such fact.

Article 124 (Conclusion of mediation) (1) Mediation shall be concluded by inserting agreements between parties in a mediation report.
  (2) Where mediation is concluded under paragraph (1), an agreement shall be deemed reached between parties with the same contents as those of the mediation report; provided, this shall not apply to matters that cannot be disposed of arbitrarily by the parties.

Article 125 (Fees) (1) Any of the following persons shall pay fees:
  1. A person who intends to register the appointment of a manager of plant variety protection under Article 4(4) or to register a change of such manager;
  2. A person who intends to apply for plant variety protection pursuant to Article 30(1);
  3. A person who intends to claim the right of priority pursuant to Article 31(1);
  4. A person who intends to file for registration under Article 52 (excluding registration of establishment of a plant variety right under Article 54);
  5. A person who intends to request adjudication on the establishment of a non-exclusive license under Article 67(1);
  6. A person who intends to file a request for trial under Article 91 or 92;
  7. A person who intends to request retrial under Article 99;
  8. A person who intends to request a certified copy, abstract copy or copy of various documents or evidence.
  (2) Fees referred to in paragraph (1), methods of payment, period of payment, and other matters shall be prescribed by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 126 (Exemption from and refund of fees) (1) Notwithstanding Article 125, the State, local governments, beneficiaries provided for in Article 5 of the National Basic Living Security Act and persons determined by the joint ministerial decree shall be exempt from fees. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) A person who intends to be exempt from fees under paragraph (1) shall submit documents prescribed by the joint ministerial decree to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) No fees shall be refunded; provided, fees paid by mistake shall be refunded at the payer's request.
  (4) Where fees are paid by mistake, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall notify the payer thereof as soon as he or she becomes aware of such fact. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) A request for a refund of fees under the proviso of paragraph (3) shall be made within three years from the date the fees are paid.

Article 127 (Language used) All documents required under this Act shall be written in Korean; where Chinese characters or other foreign characters are to be used, such shall be placed in parentheses; provided, this shall not apply where prescribed otherwise by the joint ministerial decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 128 (Keeping of documents) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall keep documents relating to an application for plant variety protection or a plant variety right for five years from the date an application for plant variety protection is abandoned, revoked or withdrawn or a decision to reject the application is made, or from the date of expiration of the plant variety right. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) Persons interested in plant variety protection may file a request with the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries for perusal or copying of documents relating to applications for plant variety protection, plant variety rights and documents relating to examinations or tests performed under Article 40 or 82 (2). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Upon receipt of a request under paragraph (2), the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall not permit perusal or copying in any of the following cases: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Where a plant variety falls under Article 56(3)2 and the applicant for the relevant plant variety protection requests non-disclosure;
  2. Where the document is related to an application for plant variety protection not laid-open.

Article 129 (Delegation of authority and entrustment of duties) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may delegate part of his or her authority under this Act to the Administrator of the Rural Development Administration, the Minister of the Korea Forest Service or the heads of affiliated agencies, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may entrust part of his or her duties under this Act to corporations or organizations relating to agriculture, forestry and fishery determined by the joint ministerial decree, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 130 (Application mutatis mutandis of the Patent Act) `Articles 217, 218 through 220 and 222 of the Patent Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the service of documents in procedures for plant variety protection and other matters.

Article 130-2 (Legal fiction as public officials in application of penalty provisions) Any of the adjudicators and the Seed Commission members who is not a public official shall be deemed to be a public official for the purposes of applying Articles 127 and 129 through 132 of the Criminal Act.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 2, 2016]

CHAPTER V PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 131 (Offense of infringement) (1) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than seven years or by a fine not exceeding 100 million won:
  1. A person who infringes on a plant variety right or exclusive license;
  2. A person who infringes on a right provided for in Article 38(1); provided, this shall only apply where the establishment of the relevant plant variety right is registered;
  3. A person who obtains a decision of plant variety protection or trial ruling by fraud or other improper means.
  (2) A prosecution of a crime under paragraph (1)1 or 2 shall be instituted only if an accusation is filed.

Article 132 (Perjury) (1) Where a witness, appraiser or interpreter who swears an oath under Article 154 or 157 of the Patent Act applied mutatis mutandis in Article 98 makes a false statement, appraisal or interpretation at the Tribunal, he or she shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than five years or by a fine not exceeding 50 million won. <Amended on Dec. 10, 2019>
  (2) If a person who commits a crime under paragraph (1) surrenders himself or herself before a decision or ruling on the case becomes final and conclusive, his or her sentence may be mitigated or remitted.

Article 133 (False labeling) A person who violates Article 89 shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won. <Amended on Sep. 10, 2019>

Article 134 (Divulgence of confidential information) A current or former public official of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries (where authority is delegated pursuant to Article 129, including a staff member of the agencies delegated with such authority) or a current or former staff member of the Tribunal who divulges or misappropriates confidential information he or she becomes aware of in the course of performing his or her duties about a plant variety for which an application is pending shall be punished by imprisonment with labor not more than five years or by a fine not exceeding 50 million won. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 135 (Joint penalty provisions) If the representative of a corporation, or an agent, or employee of or other persons employed by the corporation or an individual commits a violation under Article 131(1) or 133 in connection with the duties of the corporation or the individual, not only shall such violator be punished, but also the corporation or individual shall be punished by a fine under the relevant provisions; provided, this shall not apply where such corporation or individual has not been negligent in giving due attention and supervision concerning the relevant duties to prevent such violation.

Article 136 (Confiscation) (1) The court shall confiscate any article that constitutes an act falling under Article 131(1)1 or (2) or any article resulting from that act, or shall order the article to be given to the victim upon the victim's request.
  (2) Where the victim receives an article under paragraph (1), he or she may claim compensation for only the damage that exceeds the value of the article.

Article 137 (Administrative fine) (1) Any of the following persons shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 500 thousand won: <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  1. A person who fails to report on the intent of succession to a plant variety right, exclusive license or pledge right, or the intent of general succession, in violation of Article 62(2);
  2. A person who fails to comply with an order to report on exploitation under Article 81;
  3. A person, other than a witness, appraiser or interpreter who swears an oath under Articles 143, 259, 299 and 367 of the Civil Procedure Act applied mutatis mutandis under Article 98, who makes a false statement before the Tribunal;
  4. A person who fails to comply with an order to submit or produce documents concerning the examination or preservation of evidence issued by the Tribunal under Article 157 of the Patent Act, applied mutatis mutandis under Article 98, without justifiable grounds;
  5. A person summoned by the Tribunal as a witness, appraiser or interpreter under Article 154 or 157 of the Patent Act, applied mutatis mutandis under Article 98, who fails to comply with the summon, or refuses to swear an oath, to give testimony, or to make a statement, appraisal or interpretation without justifiable grounds.
  (2) Administrative fines under paragraph (1) shall be imposed and collected by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

